1) Physical Layer:
Provides the physical connection between devices through wires or antennas. 
Data transmission happens via electrical, optical, or wireless signals. At this layer, data is represented as raw bits — 1s and 0s.

2) Data Link Layer:
Delivers data between devices within the same local network using physical MAC addresses. 
Bits from the Physical Layer are organized into frames that contain the sender’s and receiver’s MAC addresses, the data, and a checksum for error detection. 
This layer also controls which device can transmit and when, to avoid collisions.

3) Network Layer:
Guides the packet through the network and between different networks. It uses IP addresses to decide where the packet goes and chooses the best route (routing) to reach the destination.

4) Transport Layer:
Decides how data is delivered - reliably with TCP (acknowledged delivery) or fast without guarantees with UDP. Uses ports to direct data to the right application.

5) Session Layer:
Establish connection, reconection and disconnection between machines

6) Presentation Layer:
At this layer of OSI model happens encoding(UTF-8), compression and encryption (TLS,SSL) of the packets 

7) Application Layer:
Layer where the user interacts with web browser buttons and etc.
